course: biology
lesson: 375
title: White storks: Ukraine's favourite birds and their journey to Africa
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: On top of a pole in a Ukrainian village sits a nest as wide as a kitchen table, and on it stands a white bird with black wing edges and long red legs: a white stork, leleka, back from Africa for the spring.
explain: Storks come back to the same nest year after year and add new sticks, so old nests grow huge. Granny says a stork on the roof brings luck, and for many Ukrainians the stork stands for home and spring.
explain: Storks hardly have a voice; they greet each other by throwing their heads back and clattering their bills like a wooden toy. Both parents sit on the eggs and feed the chicks.
explain: In autumn they fly about 10,000 kilometres to Africa, soaring on rising warm air instead of flapping. Warm air hardly rises over cold sea, so Ukrainian storks go round the Black Sea, not across it.
fact: Stork nests are used year after year and grow huge, sometimes about 2 metres across; sparrows often live in the nest walls, and power companies build platforms on poles so nests do not touch the wires.
fact: The white stork, leleka in Ukrainian, stands for home and spring, and Ukraine has tens of thousands of pairs, one of the biggest numbers in Europe; old people say storks never nest by a house of quarrels.
fact: Babies grow inside their mothers; the stork that delivers babies is an old European tale, perhaps because storks live close to people and return to the same nest.
fact: White storks are almost silent and clatter their bills to greet each other, while chicks hiss and mew before they learn to clatter.
fact: Storks usually lay about 3 to 5 eggs, both parents take turns sitting on them, the chicks hatch after about a month and fly at about 2 months old.
fact: On hot days storks pee onto their own legs, and the drying liquid cools their blood; the white streaks are dried wee, and vultures do the same.
fact: Storks soar on rising warm air called thermals, which hardly form over cold water, so soaring costs them far less energy than flapping.
fact: Ukrainian storks fly round the Black Sea through Turkey, the Middle East and Egypt to eastern and southern Africa, about 10,000 kilometres each way.
fact: In 1822 a stork in Germany was found with an African spear in its neck; it helped prove storks spend winter in Africa, when some people thought birds slept in mud all winter.
fact: Scientists put small GPS trackers on storks and found they use the same routes as their parents and stop at the same places.
fact: Some storks from Spain and Portugal now stay all winter and eat at rubbish dumps instead of flying to Africa.
fact: Storks eat frogs, grasshoppers, mice, worms and fish, follow tractors to catch animals turned up from the soil, and need wet meadows full of life.
fact: The shy black stork, a cousin, hides in deep forests near streams.
exercise: How big can an old stork nest grow? | About 2 metres across | About the size of a cup | About the size of a shoe | About 20 metres across
exercise: Why do power companies build platforms on poles for storks? | So the nests do not touch the wires | So storks can sell eggs | To stop storks coming back to the village every spring | So sparrows have no home
exercise: What does the white stork stand for in Ukraine? | Home and spring | War and cold winter | Gold and lots of money | Night and deep sleep
exercise: Where do babies really come from? | They grow inside their mothers | Storks bring them from Africa | They are found under cabbages | They hatch from stork eggs
exercise: How do white storks greet each other? | They clatter their bills | They sing long, sweet songs | They clap their wings loudly | They whistle through their nose
exercise: How many eggs does a white stork usually lay? | About 3 to 5 | Just 1 | About 20 eggs in a pile | About 50
exercise: Who sits on a stork's eggs? | Both parents, taking turns | Only the father, day and night | Only the mother, while he hunts | Nobody, the warm sun heats them
exercise: At what age can stork chicks fly? | About 2 months | About 2 days | About 2 years | About 2 weeks
exercise: What are the white streaks on a stork's red legs? | Dried wee that cooled its legs | White paint dripped from the roof | Bird milk for feeding the chicks | Snow brought all the way from Africa
exercise: What are thermals? | Rising columns of warm air | Warm winter feathers | Hot springs in the deserts of Africa | Wind from the sea
exercise: Why do Ukrainian storks fly round the Black Sea instead of across it? | Warm rising air hardly forms over cold water | Storks are afraid of fish | The sea is too salty to look at | It is shorter to go round the coast than straight across
exercise: How far do Ukrainian storks fly to Africa? | About 10,000 kilometres each way | About 10 kilometres | About 100 kilometres, just to the next country | About a million kilometres
exercise: What did the stork found in 1822 with an African spear in its neck help prove? | Storks spend winter in Africa | Storks are dangerous to hunters | Storks sleep in mud all winter | Storks can live with no neck at all
exercise: What did GPS trackers show about stork journeys? | They use the same routes as their parents | Every stork picks a new route each year | Storks fly straight over the sea | Storks never stop to rest
exercise: Why do some Spanish storks no longer fly to Africa? | They eat at rubbish dumps all winter | Africa is closed to storks | They forgot the way after a few years | Their wings got too small and weak for long flights
exercise: Why do storks follow tractors? | To catch animals turned up from the soil | To ride on them | To guard the farmer from foxes and wolves in the fields | Because tractors are warm
exercise: Where does the shy black stork live? | In deep forests near streams | On village roofs and church towers | In city parks | In hot deserts
test: Count them: four stork chicks poke their heads over the nest. How many beaks and legs? | 4 beaks and 8 legs | 4 beaks and 16 legs | 8 beaks and 8 legs | 4 beaks and 4 legs
test: Count them: a stork pair and their 4 chicks are on the nest. How many birds? | 6 | 4 | 5 | 8
test: Count them: a stork flies about 10,000 kilometres to Africa and the same back. How far in a year? | About 20,000 kilometres | Only about 10,000 kilometres | Only about 5,000 kilometres | About 100,000 kilometres
test: In the cartoon Dumbo, a stork flies a baby elephant to its mother in a bundle. What is right? | Babies grow inside their mothers; the stork story is an old tale | Storks really do carry baby elephants and other big babies to their mothers | Storks bring only human babies | Only circus babies come by stork
test: «my nan says storks never nest by a house of quarrels lol» What is this? | An old saying people tell, not something proved | A scientific fact about storks that was proved long ago | A law in Ukraine | A rule storks learn at school
test: «that stork is dead silent, it must be ill» What is more likely? | White storks are almost silent and clatter their bills instead | Silent storks are always sick and should be taken to a vet at once | It lost its voice from cold | It is a toy stork
test: What would happen if all the wet meadows near a village were drained and paved? | The storks would lose their frogs and worms and could leave | The storks would find more food in the gutters and the bins | The storks would eat concrete | Nothing would change for storks
test: Why is soaring cheaper for a big bird than flapping? | Rising warm air lifts it, so it hardly beats its wings | Soaring is faster than any plane, so it saves time on the journey | Flapping makes storks lighter | Big birds cannot flap at all
test: «why would a stork pee on its own legs, gross» What is the reason? | The drying liquid cools its blood on hot days | It is marking the nest so other storks keep away | It is washing its feathers | It does it by accident
test: What would happen if a big rubbish dump near a stork route closed? | Storks that ate there might start flying to Africa again | Storks would start eating rubbish at sea | Storks would stop flying at all | All storks would die at once
test: Besides an African spear, what other clue could prove where a stork spends winter? | A tracker or a ring found on the bird in Africa | Its feathers turning darker in the hot African sun | The colour of its eggs | How loud it clatters
exam: Identify the principal reason for which white storks of Ukraine circumvent the Black Sea rather than traverse it. | Rising warm air, on which they depend for soaring, scarcely forms over cold water | The sea is inhabited by large predators which seize storks as they pass over it | Storks are incapable of flight in humid air | The direct route is the longer
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the incubation of eggs by the white stork. | Both parents incubate the eggs in turn | The female alone incubates the eggs | The eggs are left to be warmed by the sun | The eggs are incubated by sparrows
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the discovery made in Germany in the year 1822? | A stork bearing an African spear demonstrated that storks winter in Africa | Storks were found to hibernate in the mud at the bottom of ponds | Storks were shown to cross the sea directly | Storks were shown to deliver infants
exam: Ascertain the function of the white deposits observed upon the legs of a white stork in hot weather. | They are dried urine, whose evaporation cools the blood | They are paint applied by farmers | They are a disease of the skin | They are remnants of eggshell
